A||||3||William||Alright Rim Refinishers... Here's how ya do it!  looooong||||1||
Z||000000||William||12-07-2000||10:49 PM||||I see a few have questions so here ya go.....Ok, here is the deal. I've done mine and you will need to be prepared to spent many many many hours to do this. I took about 30 hours to do it all!!! First,I coated the rims with gel stripper. You will need reapply several times and use putty scrapers and little wire brushes to get the clearcoat off. Go ahead and strip the interior painted parts too. If you don't, you will end up doing it again weeks later and regret that you didn't do the whole thing. (ask me how I know). Next, you will need so sand off all the corrosion and the fine lathe marks. The wheels can't be polished until ALL the lathe marks are gone! You can feel them with your fingernail. I used 230 grit sand paper and wet sanded with a hose,(sanding blocks and sanding disks on a drill depending on the area and my mood). Once the lathe marks were gone I went to 400 grit paper, then again with 600 grit paper, and again with 1000 grit paper. This is the part that took hours and hours.<P>The buffing was next. The buffer wheel is made of cloth and it is just for metal polishing. Use a large Wheel!!!!!!!!!( 6 to 7 inches). I thought a small would be good but I was wrong! A bolt goes through the middle of the wheel, and as you move the wheel to an angle to get to some areas, the bolt with scracth the rim up and you will be sanding ALL OVER AGAIN!!!!!!!You must use 2 wheels as you will be using 2 buffing compounds and each needs it's own wheel. First use a tube of Tripoli compound and buff away. This part is like magic. as you take a swipe, you will see the scrathes just disappear and the wheel turn into a mirror right before your eyes. Clean the rim with paint thinner, then do them again with another wheel and a tube of jewlers rouge for the final buffing. After the buffing you WILL NOT need aluminum polish as they will be mirrors already. You can get the metal polishing supplies at large hardware stores.<P>Next, I taped the entire face of the rim and used various exacto razor blades to cut out the openings. I painted the interior of the rim with "Plasti- kote Gun metal metallic base coat" Wow, I really like it. It is just a little lighter than the original paint and looks more like pearl than metallic to me. I then clear coated the entire rim. Warning! I actually lost some gloss using the clear coat. I tried 2 diffrent types and it seems there is no escape from this.... You will need to do this as I didn't at first and had to rebuff them in 4 months.<P>Was it worth it? I wasn't sure until I got alot of good comments at the N. FL. fiero meet, and that was before the Gun metal paint!! Have fun........ ||||reg||1
Z||000001||2xsess||12-07-2000||11:19 PM||||<B>HELL YEAH</B> it was worth it William. I've never seen factory alloys that shiny  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Hey William you want to try my wheels?lol<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||12-08-2000||03:00 AM||||Sounds like they came out very nice. Just 2 questions,<BR>1. Where's some pics?<BR>2. Why did you coat them? I would assume that without the harsh winters down there you'd have opted to leave them uncoated for the chrome like finish. (just curious)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jonathan||12-08-2000||08:57 AM||||The Eastwood company offers an wheel refinishing and buffing kit that I have heard good things about.  They also have a clearcoat specifically for newly polished metals.  Might want to check it out.  <A HREF="http://www.eastwoodcompany.com" TARGET=_blank>www.eastwoodcompany.com</A> <BR> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.eastwoodco.com/cgi-bin/sgin0101.exe?T1=13095+Z&UID=2000120808461883&UREQA=1&FNM=20" TARGET=_blank>http://www.eastwoodco.com/cgi-bin/sgin0101.exe?T1=13095+Z&UID=2000120808461883&UR EQA=1&FNM=20</A> <P>I have not tried either of these products personally.  I will probably try the clear coat when I refinish mine.  (I already have half of the other stuff)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Jonathan (edited 12-08-2000).]||||reg||1
